# Badge

<h2 id="purpose">Purpose</h2>

A badge is a small mark that shows a state or a count. Home Assistant has badges at the top of a dashboard view, one for each entity you choose, and a count beside a sidebar item. The Civica theme styles the sidebar count. It leaves the dashboard badges as Home Assistant draws them.

This page owns all four Home Assistant badges and the Civica attention count. Use a badge for new or unread items that need attention, next to the place that opens them. Do not use it for routine device totals or to label a sensor state.

This page covers:

- Badge
  - `ha-badge`
  - `ha-label-badge`
  - `hui-entity-badge`
  - `hui-state-label-badge`

<h2 id="anatomy">Anatomy</h2>

An entity badge is a pill with an optional icon, an optional small name above, and the state. An icon-only badge has no text. A label badge is a circle with the value inside and a small caption below. The sidebar count is a small red pill with a white number on the sidebar item.

<h2 id="variants">Variants, variables and extensibility</h2>

<h3 id="child-ha-badge"><code>ha-badge</code></h3>

The pill that Home Assistant draws for dashboard badges. Its properties are `type` (`badge` or `button`, default `badge`), `label` (a small caption above the content), `icon-only` and `accessible-label`. It has an `icon` slot and a default slot for the content. Home Assistant sets `type` to `button` only when the badge has an action, and then the badge can be focused.

An icon-only badge has no text, so give it a name. `accessible-label` supplies one, but only for a button badge. Home Assistant sets it to the entity name.

Variables you can set in a theme: `--badge-color` (default the secondary text colour), `--ha-badge-size` (36 pixels high and the minimum width; the radius is half the size, so it stays a pill), `--ha-badge-border-radius`, `--ha-badge-font-size` and `--ha-badge-icon-size` (18 pixels). The pill takes its background, border and shadow from the card variables, `ha-card-background`, `ha-card-border-width`, `ha-card-border-color` and `ha-card-box-shadow`. The Civica theme sets the card variables (a 1 pixel border, no shadow) and none of the `ha-badge` ones. So a Civica badge keeps its pill shape, and the theme card colours and border.

<h3 id="child-ha-label-badge"><code>ha-label-badge</code></h3>

The older round badge. Its properties are `label`, `description` and `image`; the default slot holds the value. The circle is 2.5em wide, the label is upper case white text on a coloured strip, and a long value is cut off with an ellipsis, so test it at a larger text size. Home Assistant draws it for the `ha-state-label-badge` element.

Variables: `--ha-label-badge-size`, `--ha-label-badge-font-size`, `--ha-label-badge-border-radius` (50%), `--ha-label-badge-color`, `--ha-label-badge-label-color`, `--ha-label-badge-label-text-transform`, `--label-badge-text-color` and `--label-badge-background-color`. The state label badge takes its colour from the theme’s `label-badge-red`, `label-badge-green`, `label-badge-blue`, `label-badge-yellow` and `label-badge-grey`, which Civica sets for light and dark modes. Civica sets none of the other variables. The label text stays white, so in dark mode the pale tints under it are likely to be hard to read. Test them in your own dashboard.

<h3 id="child-hui-entity-badge"><code>hui-entity-badge</code></h3>

The dashboard badge for one entity (`type: entity`, the default).

```yaml
type: entity
entity: sensor.kitchen_temperature
name: Kitchen
show_name: true
```

- `entity` is required.
- `name`, `icon`, `color` and `show_entity_picture` change the look.
- `show_name` defaults to false, and `show_state` and `show_icon` default to true. Show the name or the state in words, because an icon alone says little.
- `state_content` chooses what the state line shows.
- `tap_action`, `hold_action` and `double_tap_action` set what it does.

It draws an `ha-badge`. The badge is a button only when an action is set. An active entity uses the primary colour, an inactive one uses `state-inactive-color`, and an error uses `red-color`. A light that has its own colour uses it. A missing entity shows a “not found” message with an alert icon.

The badges sit in the view header, outside a card. Do not rely on card-mod to style them. To change a badge's shape for one entity, use a tile card with the state in text instead.

<h3 id="child-hui-state-label-badge"><code>hui-state-label-badge</code></h3>

The badge for `type: state-label`. In this version it is built on the entity badge, so it draws an `ha-badge`, not an `ha-label-badge`. It uses only `entity`, `color` and `show_name` (true by default). The `name`, `icon`, `image` and `tap_action` options are accepted but ignored. The older round label badge is a separate element, `ha-state-label-badge`.

<h3 id="sidebar-count">The Civica attention count</h3>

This is a Civica design for the count beside a sidebar item, such as notifications. It needs the Civica interface resource to be installed alongside the theme. It is not a Home Assistant badge, and it appears only in the sidebar. Switching to another theme removes it.

Geometry, set by the `civica-notification-badge-*` theme keys:

| Property | Value |
| --- | --- |
| Text | 16px, bold |
| Minimum content width | `0.75em` |
| Minimum content height | `1.25em` |
| Padding | `1px 0.25em 0` |
| Space before badge | `0.5em` |
| Radius | `10em` |
| Outline | 4px red, offset `-0.1em` |
| Foreground and fill | White on `#CA3535` |

White on #CA3535 calculates at about 5.16 to 1 from the theme colours. In dark mode the sidebar is #17191B, and the badge red against it calculates at about 3.42 to 1.

Keep the red pill and the white count whatever the theme colour. In forced colours, the outline keeps the number's boundary.

| Items | Visible badge | Accessible text |
| --- | --- | --- |
| None | No badge | The item's own name |
| 1 to 98 | The exact count | The count and what it counts |
| 99 or more | `99+` | “99 or more” and what it counts |

The visible number is hidden from screen readers, and a hidden phrase such as “3 items need attention” says the same thing once. The badge is part of the sidebar item, not an extra tab stop. A dot with no count is left as Home Assistant draws it.

Styling must not create, clear or acknowledge a notification. The theme cannot decide that something has been read. Do not announce every background change.

<h2 id="limits">Advantages and limitations</h2>

- Entity badges are native and follow the theme colours.
- The sidebar count is clear and says its number in words.
- You cannot change the shape of one entity badge with a theme variable.
- The Civica theme does not set any `ha-badge` or `ha-label-badge` variable.
- An icon-only badge has no text. A long label badge value is cut off.
- The attention count works only in the sidebar and needs the Civica interface resource.

<h2 id="usage">Usage policy and cognitive accessibility</h2>

A count has a different job from a status. A [tag](tag.html) names a state, and a [notification banner](notification-banners.html) carries a message. A count cannot say how serious the items are, so keep the destination clear.

Show a name or a state in words on an entity badge. Avoid counts nobody has explained.

Success criteria that apply:

- 1.4.1 Use of Colour: show a name, icon or state, not colour alone.
- 1.3.1 Info and Relationships: the count is in the item's name.
- 2.5.3 Label in Name: the visible text is in the accessible name.
- 4.1.3 Status Messages: announce a count once, not on every change.
- 1.4.4 Resize Text and 1.4.10 Reflow: check long label badge values.

Check counts 0, 1, 9, 10, 98, 99 and 100, text at 200%, one announcement, keyboard use, live changes, forced colours, every theme colour and the restore when you switch away from Civica.

